<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonard Bernstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Louis Bernstein, Lenny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonard Bernstein was an American conductor, composer, pianist and music educator, born in Lawrence, Massachusetts, in 1918 to Jewish immigrant parents, and the first American-born conductor to win international acclaim. His unrehearsed debut with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Philharmonic in 1943, broadcast nationwide, made him famous, and he was its music director from 1957, jointly at first, until 1969. He composed the musical West Side Story (1957), On the Town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a> Psalms and the score of On the Waterfront, and he was central to the modern revival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a>'s music. His Young People's Concerts brought classical music to television audiences. He died in New York in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonard-bernstein.jpg" alt="Leonard Bernstein" width="330" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jack Mitchell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonard_Bernstein_by_Jack_Mitchell_(high_quality).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leonard Bernstein was born Louis Bernstein on 25 August 1918 in Lawrence, Massachusetts, to Jewish immigrant parents. His father, Samuel Joseph Bernstein, came from an ultra-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> shtetl in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, wanted to become a rabbi and left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1908. His mother, Jennie, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, arrived in 1905 and worked in the woolen mills of Lawrence. In 1935 he enrolled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard College</a>, where he studied music with Edward Burlingame Hill and Walter Piston, and his senior thesis was titled The Absorption of Race Elements into American Music. At Harvard he led a performance of Marc Blitzstein's recently banned musical The Cradle Will Rock in 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bernstein moved to New York City, where he coached singers, taught piano and transcribed jazz and pop music under the pseudonym Lenny Amber. In 1943 his last-minute debut with the New York Philharmonic at Carnegie Hall, broadcast live across the country, made him widely known overnight. He soon became the first American-born, American-trained conductor to achieve international standing, at a time when conductors traditionally came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In the 1950s he wrote five new works for Broadway, composed the score for Elia Kazan's On the Waterfront in 1954 and was appointed music director of the New York Philharmonic, with which he toured the world.</p><p>He led the orchestra for eleven seasons and championed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a>, Carl Nielsen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-sibelius">Jean Sibelius</a> and Charles Ives. He conducted over 40 world premieres, among them the first performance of Ives's Symphony No. 2. His works include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a>, West Side Story, the Chichester Psalms of 1965 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> piece Mass of 1971, and his Jewish heritage is audible in the symphonies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> and Kaddish. On television he introduced millions of viewers to symphonic music, notably through the Young People's Concerts. In the 1980s he co-founded the Los Angeles Philharmonic Institute, the Schleswig-Holstein Musik Festival Orchestral Academy and the Pacific Music Festival.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bernstein supported civil rights, protested against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, advocated nuclear disarmament and raised money for AIDS research. On Christmas Day 1989 he conducted Beethoven's Symphony No. 9 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> to celebrate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a>. He announced his retirement from conducting on 9 October 1990 and died five days later at the Dakota in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, aged 72, of a heart attack brought on by a pleural tumour. He is buried at Green-Wood Cemetery in Brooklyn, next to his wife Felicia Montealegre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leonard Bernstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>1; <b>Leonard Bernstein</b> used it to teach counterpoint on television; and the Beatles sang its title under the last verse of &quot;Paperback Writer&quot; (1966).</blockquote><a href="/songs/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques</a></li><li><blockquote>The musicologist Alexander Rehding notes that scholarly consensus finds no factual basis for the idea, and that even <b>Leonard Bernstein</b>, who used &quot;Freiheit&quot; in two performances in 1989, called it conjecture.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lawrence, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.71,-71.16&amp;year=1964" aria-label="Lawrence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2829 646 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leonard Bernstein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3279 916h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3279 916h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.71,-71.16&amp;year=1964">What was here in 1964? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
